    47	#   `out = np.full_like(g, bg)` is still useful as an intermediate core canvas,
    48	#   but returning that full-size canvas is no longer correct because D8 now
    49	#   demands a tight crop. The original "erase rule examples" assumption also no
    50	#   longer holds; we must explicitly rebuild them as a moved legend strip.
    51	#   The preservation loop for bystanders remains valid, but only for colors that
    52	#   are not part of any learned source color.
    53	# - Placement legality:
    54	#   The generator enforces four non-overlap regimes: top-edge legends do not
    55	#   overlap each other, bottom-edge source pixels are 4-neighbor separated,
    56	#   projected middle-row stencil footprints do not overlap, and bystanders avoid
    57	#   both the input legend/source neighborhoods and the projected footprints.
    58	#   Stencils are generated with their anchor on the stencil's top row, which
    59	#   guarantees that a downward-pointing top-edge legend always fits.
    60	# - Visual observability:
    61	#   Across examples, humans can see three stable cues: rule examples live on the
    62	#   input top edge, source controls live on the input bottom edge, and the
    63	#   output foreground appears in a central band with the rule examples reissued
    64	#   as a legend strip at the output top edge. Tight crop then removes the dead
    65	#   border, so the changed roles of "edge as control" and "edge as legend" are
    66	#   both visible.
    67	# - Neatness / color inference:
    68	#   Source colors and target colors still come only from local legends, so color
    69	#   inference is structural rather than numeric. The generator keeps bystanders
    70	#   on colors outside all rule source colors and keeps projected stencil
    71	#   footprints disjoint, preventing the center from becoming visually tangled.
    72	# - New assumptions introduced here and matched by the generator:
    73	#   1. A true legend is a top-edge source->target vertical pair with a nearby
    74	#      teal stencil below it.
    75	#   2. Activatable non-legend source pixels lie on the border; the generator
    76	#      uses the bottom border subset to keep the projection rule obvious.
    77	#   3. The moved output legend is packed left-to-right in the same visible order
    78	#      as the input legends.
    79	#   4. Tight crop is taken after combining the relocated legend strip with the
    80	#      projected central painting.
